  "live_records": {
    "q-280970": {
      "dom_id": "step-q280970",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The remainder of dividing $p(x)=9{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4}-2x-\\frac{1}{3}$ by $1-3x$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by $(1-3x)=-3(x-\\frac{1}{3}),$ the remainder equals $p(\\frac{1}{3}).$ \n\nEvaluating $p(\\frac{1}{3})$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\frac{1}{3}) & =9\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}-3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the remainder of dividing $p(x)=9{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4}-2x-\\frac{1}{3}$ by $1-3x$ is $-1.$\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{5}$ | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $9$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n|  | $9$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
