    "q-7626": {
      "dom_id": "step-q7626",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the remainder when $p(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+4$ is divided by $5-x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by $(5-x)=-(x-5),$ the remainder equals $p(5).$ \n\nEvaluating $p(5)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(5) & =2\\cdot {5}^{2}-4\\cdot 5+4 \\\\  & =50-20+4 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $p(x)$ is divided by $(5-x)$ the remainder is $34.$\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $10$ | $30$ |\n|  | $2$ | $6$ | $34$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
